    {
      "filename": "Bighorn National Recreation Area, Wyoming and Montana (0964f1bf-54a9-4eab-bd09-8da7baa01b52).jpg",
      "title": "File:Bighorn National Recreation Area, Wyoming and Montana (0964f1bf-54a9-4eab-bd09-8da7baa01b52).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/53/Bighorn_National_Recreation_Area%2C_Wyoming_and_Montana_%280964f1bf-54a9-4eab-bd09-8da7baa01b52%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bighorn_National_Recreation_Area,_Wyoming_and_Montana_(0964f1bf-54a9-4eab-bd09-8da7baa01b52).jpg",
      "width": 3072,
      "height": 2048,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "NPS staff",
      "description": "Cliffs alongside the lake\nBighorn Canyon National Recreation Area was established by an act of Congress on October 15, 1966, following the construction of the Yellowtail Dam by the Bureau of Reclamation. This dam, named after the famous Crow chairman Robert Yellowtail, harnessed the waters of the Bighorn River and turned this variable stream into a magnificent lake. \nBighorn Lake extends approximately 60 miles through Wyoming and Montana, 55 miles of which are held within spectacular Bighorn Canyon. The Recreation Area is composed of 70,000+ acres, which straddles the northern Wyoming and southern Montana borders. There are two visitor centers and other developed facilities in Fort Smith, Montana and near Lovell, Wyoming. The Afterbay Lake below the Yellowtail Dam is a good spot for trout fishing and wildlife viewing for ducks, geese and other animals. The Bighorn River below the Afterbay Dam is a world class trout fishing area.\nKeywords: bica; Recreation areas; National parks and reserves; Canyons; Lakes",
      "dateCreated": "Taken on 9 December 2003",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "bighorn-canyon-national-recreation-area-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "c850",
